sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2014-tumor-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Raising extracellular chloride, which hyperpolarizes cells through native chloride channels, decreases the incidence of KRASG12D-induced tumor-like structures in Xenopus laevis embryos by about 38%, showing that tumor suppression depends on the change in membrane voltage rather than on the Kv1.5 channel or on potassium specifically.";
    dc:type "experimental-result";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "High-chloride hyperpolarization suppresses KRAS tumors, so the effect is ion-independent";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Chernet BT, Levin M, “Transmembrane voltage potential of somatic cells controls oncogene-mediated tumorigenesis at long-range”, Oncotarget (2014), doi:10.18632/oncotarget.1935; section “RESULTS › Long-range hyperpolarization suppresses the formation of ITLS”. Scope: Xenopus laevis embryos injected with KRASG12D mRNA and raised in elevated (70 mM) chloride medium"
}
